UPDATE editorial_documents SET document = '{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Luis Días Portorreal, universally known as Luis \"El Terror\" Días, was a Dominican composer, guitarist, singer and bandleader, born on June 21, 1952 in Bonao and dead on December 8, 2009 in Santo Domingo. He is the figure Dominican musicians point to when they want to argue that the country’s folklore and its rock could be the same music. More than three hundred of his compositions were recorded by other artists, he is widely called the father of Dominican rock, and in 2004 the Dominican state declared him Patrimonio Cultural de la Nación while he was still alive to hear it.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Bonao and Convite","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Días grew up inside Dominican vernacular music: his father played the tres, his mother sang salves. He studied with the teachers Juan Zorrilla and Tatán Jiménez, and at sixteen formed his first group, Los Chonnys, playing rock and roll in Bonao. In 1970 he moved to Santo Domingo to read psychology at the Universidad Autónoma de Santo Domingo.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"In 1972, at the initiative of the sociologist Dagoberto Tejeda, he joined Convite as guitarist and vocalist. Convite was less a band than a research project with instruments — an effort to find, document and perform rhythms that had survived in corners of the island without ever reaching a recording studio. That fieldwork is the foundation everything else in his career was built on. Convite played the Festival Internacional de la Nueva Canción \"Siete Días con el Pueblo\" in 1974, where his \"Obrero acepta mi mano\" served as the official theme.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Madora and Transporte Urbano","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"He left Convite in 1978 and formed Madora with Gustavo Moré and others, beginning the experiments that fused jazz with Antillean folklore. Between 1980 and 1982 he taught Dominican music workshops at the American Museum of Natural History in New York.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"The decisive group was Transporte Urbano, formed in 1982 with Juan Francisco Ordóñez on guitar, Guy Frómeta on drums, José Duluc on percussion and Bruno Ranson on saxophone. It is generally treated as the birth of Dominican rock: not Dominicans playing rock, but rock built from the inside out of merengue, bachata, mangulina, salve and more than forty other Dominican and Haitian rhythms, crossed with reggae, jazz and blues. He gave the results names of his own — merenrock, tecnoamargue — and released albums including Luis \"Terror\" Días (1984), Merenrock (1988), El Accidente (1998), La Yola (2000), Radio Macana (2003) and Tiempo de Ocio, issued in the year he died.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"The songbook","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"For all the experimentation, his widest reach came through other people’s voices. Among the best known:","type":"text"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"\"Carnaval,\" better known as \"Baila en la calle\" — the 1984 Dominican carnival theme, carried by ","type":"text"},{"type":"artistReference","attrs":{"artistId":"bc310977-31a9-41bb-9af2-7d3a0d7fabdd","displayText":"Fernando Villalona","occurrenceId":"005ca120-6139-4e1c-a1ae-37b9370a2050"}},{"text":" and ","type":"text"},{"type":"artistReference","attrs":{"artistId":"2cc97ca9-126d-48c5-922f-e9d5c8b0360d","displayText":"Sonia Silvestre","occurrenceId":"f2e1d19b-8e87-417c-bec1-faa2354b3651"}},{"text":",","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"\"Marola,\" \"Las vampiras\" and \"La novia\" — ","type":"text