UPDATE editorial_documents SET document = '{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Ricardo Alberto Mateo Alcántara","type":"text"},{"text":" (March 31, 1959 – September 29, 2013), popularly known as ","type":"text"},{"text":"Richie Ricardo","type":"text"},{"text":" or ","type":"text"},{"text":"\"El Natural,\"","type":"text"},{"text":" was a prominent Dominican merengue singer, radio host, and DJ. He was one of the most charismatic figures of the golden age of merengue during the 1980s.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Early Life and Career Beginnings","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Born in the community of Pajonal, in the municipality of Las Matas de Farfán, San Juan Province, Dominican Republic, he showed artistic talent from a young age. Before pursuing singing full-time, he worked as a professional radio announcer and DJ at stations such as Radio Clarín.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Because his vocal timbre naturally resembled that of ","type":"text"},{"type":"artistReference","attrs":{"artistId":"3f8bafec-e5ee-415d-8405-9551cceeeb9b","displayText":"Johnny Ventura","occurrenceId":"42cc6e1e-b71d-41eb-b0e6-7c0030a4ee72"}},{"text":", he was frequently accused of imitating him. To establish his own identity and emphasize that his vocal tone was entirely authentic, he adopted the slogan and stage name ","type":"text"},{"text":"\"El Natural.\"","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Breakthrough and Peak Years (1986–1988)","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"In 1986, he made his official recording debut with the album ","type":"text"},{"text":"El Potro","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":", released under the Discum label and executive produced by Luis Medrano. The album''s title was a subtle homage to ","type":"text"},{"type":"artistReference","attrs":{"artistId":"3f8bafec-e5ee-415d-8405-9551cceeeb9b","displayText":"Johnny Ventura","occurrenceId":"27c69501-b830-4fe2-81ca-6a2df470815c"}},{"text":" (\"El Caballo\").","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"In 1987, he signed with Kubaney Records and released his second album, solidifying his popularity on Dominican radio and stage circuits. That same year, he received his sole Casandra Award nomination for Musical Ensemble of the Year.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"His most notable hits include:","type":"text"}]},{"type":"bulletList","content":[{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"\"Señor Pulpero\"","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"\"La negra se movía\"","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" (adapted by ","type":"text"},{"type":"artistReference","attrs":{"artistId":"001831dd-3baa-4512-88f5-f420ec7c2619","displayText":"Pochy Familia","occurrenceId":"d81e4bdf-3b84-4e5c-b0a0-d883b9b71f5a"}},{"text":" from ","type":"text"},{"text":"\"La hierba se movía\"","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":")","type":"text"}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"\"Casimiro\"","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"\"El funeral\"","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"\"Catalina\"","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"\"El hot dog\"","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]}]}]},{"type":"listItem","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"\"Richie merengue boogaloo\"","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"His orchestra served as an early platform for several musicians and arrangers who later became major figures in the industry, including ","type":"text"},{"type":"artistReference","attrs":{"artistId":"001831dd-3baa-4512-88f5-f420ec7c2619","displayText":"Pochy Familia","occurrenceId":"bc6ef116-f231-4d85-af42-557aa5bbb611"}},{"text":" (who arranged several of his hits), ","type":"text"},{"type":"artistReference","attrs":{"artistId":"c73737c2-0106-4a87-8dbe-5f1650d34342","displayText":"Kinito Méndez","occurrenceId":"6648a258-6d18-47c0-8fd9-f8577ac2ea0c"}},{"text":" (who sold him his first two compositions, ","type":"text"},{"text":"\"Catalina\"","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" and ","type":"text"},{"text":"\"Casimiro\"","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":"), Bobby Rafael, Freddy Gerardo, and percussionist Paco Quero.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Later Years and Retirement","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"By 1989, the orchestra began losing commercial momentum amid competition from other major acts. In the early 1990s, he released the album ","type":"text"},{"text":"El Natural regresa ''93","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" under Jean Records.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Faced with financial challenges in maintaining a 13-piece band and persistent false rumors regarding his health, he released the album ","type":"text"},{"text":"Vivito y coleando","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" in 1996, featuring the lead track ","type":"text"},{"text":"\"El funeral.\"","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"italic"}]},{"text":" Shortly after, he sold his musical equipment, retired from touring, and returned to radio broadcasting.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Passing","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Richie Ricardo passed away at age 54 on the night of September 29, 2013, in Santo Domingo, following a myocardial infarction while playing dominoes with friends and family. He was buried in the municipal cemetery of his hometown, Las Matas de Farfán. He had no children.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Musical Style and Legacy","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"His musical style combined fast-paced 1980s merengue rhythms with humorous lyrics and Dominican folklore, marked by interactive, charismatic stage performances.","type":"text"}]}]}'::jsonb, revision = revision - 1, updated_at = now() WHERE id = (SELECT d.id FROM editorial_documents d JOIN artists a ON a.id = d.owner_artist_id WHERE a.slug = 'richie-ricardo' AND d.locale = 'en' AND d.document_type = 'artist_biography');
UPDATE artists SET bio_en = 'Ricardo Alberto Mateo Alcántara (March 31, 1959 – September 29, 2013), popularly known as Richie Ricardo or "El Natural," was a prominent Dominican merengue singer, radio host, and DJ. He was one of the most charismatic figures of the golden age of merengue during the 1980s.

**Early Life and Career Beginnings**

Born in the community of Pajonal, in the municipality of Las Matas de Farfán, San Juan Province, Dominican Republic, he showed artistic talent from a young age. Before pursuing singing full-time, he worked as a professional radio announcer and DJ at stations such as Radio Clarín.

Because his vocal timbre naturally resembled that of Johnny Ventura, he was frequently accused of imitating him. To establish his own identity and emphasize that his vocal tone was entirely authentic, he adopted the slogan and stage name "El Natural."

**Breakthrough and Peak Years (1986–1988)**

In 1986, he made his official recording debut with the album El Potro, released under the Discum label and executive produced by Luis Medrano. The album''s title was a subtle homage to Johnny Ventura ("El Caballo").

In 1987, he signed with Kubaney Records and released his second album, solidifying his popularity on Dominican radio and stage circuits. That same year, he received his sole Casandra Award nomination for Musical Ensemble of the Year.

His most notable hits include:

- "Señor Pulpero"  
- "La negra se movía" (adapted by Pochy Familia from "La hierba se movía")  
- "Casimiro"  
- "El funeral"  
- "Catalina"  
- "El hot dog"  
- "Richie merengue boogaloo"

His orchestra served as an early platform for several musicians and arrangers who later became major figures in the industry, including Pochy Familia (who arranged several of his hits), Kinito Méndez (who sold him his first two compositions, "Catalina" and "Casimiro"), Bobby Rafael, Freddy Gerardo, and percussionist Paco Quero.

**Later Years and Retirement**

By 1989, the orchestra began losing commercial momentum amid competition from other major acts. In the early 1990s, he released the album El Natural regresa ''93 under Jean Records.

Faced with financial challenges in maintaining a 13-piece band and persistent false rumors regarding his health, he released the album Vivito y coleando in 1996, featuring the lead track "El funeral." Shortly after, he sold his musical equipment, retired from touring, and returned to radio broadcasting.

**Passing**

Richie Ricardo passed away at age 54 on the night of September 29, 2013, in Santo Domingo, following a myocardial infarction while playing dominoes with friends and family. He was buried in the municipal cemetery of his hometown, Las Matas de Farfán. He had no children.

**Musical Style and Legacy**

His musical style combined fast-paced 1980s merengue rhythms with humorous lyrics and Dominican folklore, marked by interactive, charismatic stage performances.' WHERE slug = 'richie-ricardo';
