ype":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"A Son de Pop y primeros lanzamientos","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Aunque ya interpretaba música en obras de teatro y presentaciones especiales, lanzó formalmente su proyecto discográfico como solista con el álbum A Son de Pop (2017). El disco incluyó sencillos como \"Entregarte todo\" y \"Prohibido quererte\", los cuales lograron una gran acogida radial y en plataformas digitales.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Capítulo 2 y nominación al Latin Grammy","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"En 2019 lanzó su segundo álbum de estudio, Capítulo 2, consolidando su propuesta lírica y musical centrada en el desamor, la vulnerabilidad y la superación personal.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Posteriormente, en 2021 presentó el álbum Que Me Firme El Cielo. La producción incluyó el sencillo \"Que me firme el cielo\", tema que le valió una nominación a los Premios Latin Grammy en la categoría de Mejor Canción Tropical, marcando un hito importante en su proyección internacional.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Evolución y estilo","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Su propuesta musical se caracteriza por la fusión del pop y la balada romántica con matices de géneros tropicales como la bachata y el merengue. Ha destacado por su capacidad narrativa al componer, así como por la potencia y expresividad de su interpretación vocal.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Reconocimientos y legado","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"A lo largo de su trayectoria, ha sido galardonada con múltiples reconocimientos en los Premios Soberano de la República Dominicana, destacándose en categorías como Cantante Solista y Espectáculo del Año. Es considerada una de las figuras más influyentes del pop dominicano contemporáneo.","type":"text"}]}]}'::jsonb, revision = revision - 1, updated_at = now() WHERE id = (SELECT d.id FROM editorial_documents d JOIN artists a ON a.id = d.owner_artist_id WHERE a.slug = 'techy-fatule' AND d.locale = 'es' AND d.document_type = 'artist_biography');
UPDATE artists SET bio_es = 'Stephanie Fatule Báez (Santo Domingo, República Dominicana, 26 de febrero de 1987), conocida artísticamente como Techy Fatule, es una destacada cantante, compositora y actriz dominicana. Reconocida por su estilo dentro del pop latino, la balada y la música tropical, ha logrado consolidarse como una de las voces contemporáneas más versátiles de la música dominicana.

**Primeros años y formación**

Es hija del reconocido comunicador, actor y cantante Carlos Alfredo Fatule y de la presentadora de televisión y locutora Tatiana Rosario. Creció en un entorno estrechamente vinculado a los medios de comunicación y las artes, lo que despertó su interés por el entretenimiento desde temprana edad.

Inició su formación artística estudiando canto, actuación y piano. Complementó sus estudios musicales en la prestigiosa institución Berklee College of Music, lo que le brindó una sólida base técnica en composición e interpretación.

**Carrera actoral y televisión**

Antes de enfocarse por completo en la música, Techy desarrolló una activa carrera en el teatro, el cine y la televisión dominicana. Participó en diversas producciones teatrales y musicales como Había una vez en Nueva York, Evita y Dreamgirls.

En el ámbito audiovisual, formó parte del elenco de varias películas dominicanas y condujo programas de televisión y radio, destacándose por su carisma y versatilidad escénica.

**Carrera musical**

A Son de Pop y primeros lanzamientos

Aunque ya interpretaba música en obras de teatro y presentaciones especiales, lanzó formalmente su proyecto discográfico como solista con el álbum A Son de Pop (2017). El disco incluyó sencillos como "Entregarte todo" y "Prohibido quererte", los cuales lograron una gran acogida radial y en plataformas digitales.

Capítulo 2 y nominación al Latin Grammy

En 2019 lanzó su segundo álbum de estudio, Capítulo 2, consolidando su propuesta lírica y musical centrada en el desamor, la vulnerabilidad y la superación personal.

Posteriormente, en 2021 presentó el álbum Que Me Firme El Cielo. La producción incluyó el sencillo "Que me firme el cielo", tema que le valió una nominación a los Premios Latin Grammy en la categoría de Mejor Canción Tropical, marcando un hito importante en su proyección internacional.

**Evolución y estilo**

Su propuesta musical se caracteriza por la fusión del pop y la balada romántica con matices de géneros tropicales como la bachata y el merengue. Ha destacado por su capacidad narrativa al componer, así como por la potencia y expresividad de su interpretación vocal.

**Reconocimientos y legado**

A lo largo de su trayectoria, ha sido galardonada con múltiples reconocimientos en los Premios Soberano de la República Dominicana, destacándose en categorías como Cantante Solista y Espectáculo del Año. Es considerada una de las figuras más influyentes del pop dominicano contemporáneo.' WHERE slug = 'techy-fatule';
DELETE FROM editorial_entity_references WHERE editorial_document_id = (SELECT d.id FROM editorial_documents d JOIN artists a ON a.id = d.owner_artist_id WHERE a.slug = 'techy-fatule' AND d.locale = 'es' AND d.document_type = 'artist_biography');

UPDATE editorial_documents SET document = '{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Tercer Cielo is one of the most successful and influential Christian music acts to emerge from the Dominican Republic, a duo whose blend of contemporary production, genuine faith, and accessible melodies has won them a devoted following throughout the Spanish-speaking world. The group is composed of Juan Carlos Rodríguez, a composer and producer, and Evelyn Herrera, a vocalist and songwriter of Mexican-American descent, who built their musical ministry from roots in Dominican evangelical culture and rose to continental recognition across the United States, Latin America, and beyond.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"The Origins: Rodríguez and Yaroide","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Tercer Cielo was born in the year 2000 in Santo Domingo, when composer Juan Carlos Rodríguez invited vocalist ","type":"text"},{"type":"artistReference","attrs":{"artistId":"629a7daa-5ab3-4b77-825f-6795b8c4a566","displayText":"Marcos Yaroide","occurrenceId":"0e20e760-b808-4a20-a9e0-5dda8200d2a0"}},{"text":" to collaborate on creating contemporary Christian music with Latin pop sounds. The duo began performing at local Christian events, churches, and benefit concerts, gradually gaining recognition within the Dominican evangelical community. Their first single, \"Ella y él,\" generated enough resonance to justify the recording of a full-length album.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"First Era: Rodríguez and Yaroide (2000-2005)","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Their debut album, \"En ti\" (2000), established the duo''s musical proposal: songs with Christian content and contemporary arrangements incorporating influences from Latin pop, R&B, and urban music. Soon after came the album \"Mi primer amor\" (2002), which achieved significant commercial success with two gold records and multiple recognitions within the Latin American Christian music circuit.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"During this period, Tercer Cielo positioned itself as one of the leading voices of contemporary Christian music in Spanish, with compositions that balanced messages of faith, redemption, and spiritual transformation with catchy melodies designed to connect with a young audience.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"The Transition: Evelyn Herrera Joins","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"In 2006, Marcos Yaroide made the decision to leave the group to pursue theological studies and dedicate himself to pastoral work, a change that seemed to threaten the project''s continuity. However, Juan Carlos Rodríguez was invited to participate in producing a musical project for American vocalist Evelyn Herrera, born April 10, 1985, in Phoenix, Arizona. This professional collaboration naturally evolved into a personal partnership: Rodríguez and Herrera married in January 2006, at a moment when both were shaping the transformation of the duo.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Evelyn Herrera, of Mexican-American heritage, brought her own voice, songwriting talent, and perspective that helped reinvent the sound and reach of the project. Her formal incorporation into the duo in 2007 marked the beginning of what would become Tercer Cielo''s most successful years.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Second Era: Rodríguez and Herrera (2007-Present)","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"With Evelyn Herrera now integrated, Tercer Cielo significantly expanded its repertoire and commercial appeal. The duo began including not only Christian-content material but also secular love songs that broadened their listener base without compromising the spiritual message that defined them. This balance allowed their compositions to resonate in both religious contexts and mainstream Latin commercial radio.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Their 2009 album, \"Gente común, sueños extraordinarios,\" represented the peak of this expansion period. The production reached number one on the Billboard Latin Pop Albums chart in September 2009 and received RIAA gold certification. Commercial success was accompanied by international recognition: nominations for the Latin Grammy Award in the category Best Christian Album in Spanish, along with recognitions at the Premios Lo Nuestro and Latin Billboard Music Awards.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Iconic Songs and Sonic Legacy","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Tercer Cielo''s catalog includes some of the most recognizable songs of contemporary Latin Christian music. \"El uno para el otro\" became an anthem for Christian couples and weddings. \"Yo te extrañaré\" conquered streaming playlists with its combination of romantic melancholy and spiritual depth. \"Mi último día,\" \"Creeré,\" and \"Tu amor no es de este mundo\" demonstrated the duo''s ability to address themes of faith from diverse emotional and narrative angles. Songs like \"No importa\" won AMCL awards in 2007 for Song and Composition of the Year, while \"Mi último día\" received the AMCL Song of the Year Award in 2009, and \"Tu amor no es de este mundo\" was recognized in 2011.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Commercial Impact and Global Reach","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Tercer Cielo has sold more than 1.5 million albums throughout the Spanish-speaking world, with particularly strong presence in Mexico, Central America, the Caribbean, and Latino communities in the United States. Their concerts have reached more than 200 cities around the world, solidifying them as one of the most accessible and broadly reaching Christian acts in the Latin market.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Ministry Beyond Music","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"From its inception, Tercer Cielo has understood its career as an integral ministry. Beyond recordings and concerts, the duo has participated in evangelism projects, charity events, and has used its platform to promote Christian values of family, faith, and personal transformation. At the 2011 Billboard Latin Music Awards, Rodríguez and Herrera served as presenters, legitimizing themselves as representative figures of the Christian Latino space in the commercial music industry.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Retirement from Live Performance","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"In February 2023, Juan Carlos Rodríguez and Evelyn Herrera announced their retirement from live musical performances, prioritizing family life over touring commitments. In their statement, the duo expressed that family is their \"first ministry,\" reflecting a decision that privileges personal stability over professional visibility. However, both committed to continuing studio recording, maintaining their creative output in a format that allows for greater flexibility.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Legacy","type":"text","marks":[{"type":"bold"}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Gente común, sueños extraordinarios reached number one on the Billboard Latin Pop Albums chart in September 2009 and was certified gold by the RIAA. A Dominican Christian act topping a mainstream chart had not happened before, and it is why the pair were invited to present at the Billboard Latin Music Awards in 2011.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"Two songs got away from them, which is the part that lasts. El uno para el otro became standard repertoire at Christian weddings. Yo te extrañaré left the church altogether: it is sung at funerals across the Spanish-speaking world, often by people who do not know whose it is or that it is a Christian song.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"text":"The duo also left a second career behind it. ","type":"text"},{"type":"artistReference","attrs":{"artistId":"629a7daa-5ab3-4b77-825f-6795b8c4a566","displayText":"Marcos Yaroide","occurrenceId":"b6e070d6-bf50-484d-a539-a2f455611a96"}},{"text":", the voice of the first era, who stepped away in 2006 to study theology, is now a central figure in Dominican worship in his own right.","type":"text"}]}]}'::jsonb, revision = revision - 1, updated_at = now() WHERE id = (SELECT d.id FROM editorial_documents d JOIN artists a ON a.id = d.owner_artist_id WHERE a.slug = 'tercer-cielo' AND d.locale = 'en' AND d.document_type = 'artist_biography');
UPDATE artists SET bio_en = 'Tercer Cielo is one of the most successful and influential Christian music acts to emerge from the Dominican Republic, a duo whose blend of contemporary production, genuine faith, and accessible melodies has won them a devoted following throughout the Spanish-speaking world. The group is composed of Juan Carlos Rodríguez, a composer and producer, and Evelyn Herrera, a vocalist and songwriter of Mexican-American descent, who built their musical ministry from roots in Dominican evangelical culture and rose to continental recognition across the United States, Latin America, and beyond.

**The Origins: Rodríguez and Yaroide**

Tercer Cielo was born in the year 2000 in Santo Domingo, when composer Juan Carlos Rodríguez invited vocalist Marcos Yaroide to collaborate on creating contemporary Christian music with Latin pop sounds. The duo began performing at local Christian events, churches, and benefit concerts, gradually gaining recognition within the Dominican evangelical community. Their first single, "Ella y él," generated enough resonance to justify the recording of a full-length album.

**First Era: Rodríguez and Yaroide (2000-2005)**

Their debut album, "En ti" (2000), established the duo''s musical proposal: songs with Christian content and contemporary arrangements incorporating influences from Latin pop, R&B, and urban music. Soon after came the album "Mi primer amor" (2002), which achieved significant commercial success with two gold records and multiple recognitions within the Latin American Christian music circuit.

During this period, Tercer Cielo positioned itself as one of the leading voices of contemporary Christian music in Spanish, with compositions that balanced messages of faith, redemption, and spiritual transformation with catchy melodies designed to connect with a young audience.

**The Transition: Evelyn Herrera Joins**

In 2006, Marcos Yaroide made the decision to leave the group to pursue theological studies and dedicate himself to pastoral work, a change that seemed to threaten the project''s continuity. However, Juan Carlos Rodríguez was invited to participate in producing a musical project for American vocalist Evelyn Herrera, born April 10, 1985, in Phoenix, Arizona. This professional collaboration naturally evolved into a personal partnership: Rodríguez and Herrera married in January 2006, at a moment when both were shaping the transformation of the duo.

Evelyn Herrera, of Mexican-American heritage, brought her own voice, songwriting talent, and perspective that helped reinvent the sound and reach of the project. Her formal incorporation into the duo in 2007 marked the beginning of what would become Tercer Cielo''s most successful years.

**Second Era: Rodríguez and Herrera (2007-Present)**

With Evelyn Herrera now integrated, Tercer Cielo significantly expanded its repertoire and commercial appeal. The duo began including not only Christian-content material but also secular love songs that broadened their listener base without compromising the spiritual message that defined them. This balance allowed their compositions to resonate in both religious contexts and mainstream Latin commercial radio.

Their 2009 album, "Gente común, sueños extraordinarios," represented the peak of this expansion period. The production reached number one on the Billboard Latin Pop Albums chart in September 2009 and received RIAA gold certification. Commercial success was accompanied by international recognition: nominations for the Latin Grammy Award in the category Best Christian Album in Spanish, along with recognitions at the Premios Lo Nuestro and Latin Billboard Music Awards.

**Iconic Songs and Sonic Legacy**

Tercer Cielo''s catalog includes some of the most recognizable songs of contemporary Latin Christian music. "El uno para el otro" became an anthem for Christian couples and weddings. "Yo te extrañaré" conquered streaming playlists with its combination of romantic melancholy and spiritual depth. "Mi último día," "Creeré," and "Tu amor no es de este mundo" demonstrated the duo''s ability to address themes of faith from diverse emotional and narrative angles. Songs like "No importa" won AMCL awards in 2007 for Song and Composition of the Year, while "Mi último día" received the AMCL Song of the Year Award in 2009, and "Tu amor no es de este mundo" was recognized in 2011.

**Commercial Impact and Global Reach**

Tercer Cielo has sold more than 1.5 million albums throughout the Spanish-speaking world, with particularly strong presence in Mexico, Central America, the Caribbean, and Latino communities in the United States. Their concerts have reached more than 200 cities around the world, solidifying them as one of the most accessible and broadly reaching Christian acts in the Latin market.

**Ministry Beyond Music**

From its inception, Tercer Cielo has understood its career as an integral ministry. Beyond recordings and concerts, the duo has participated in evangelism projects, charity events, and has used its platform to promote Christian values of family, faith, and personal transformation. At the 2011 Billboard Latin Music Awards, Rodríguez and Herrera served as presenters, legitimizing themselves as representative figures of the Christian Latino space in the commercial music industry.

**Retirement from Live Performance**

In February 2023, Juan Carlos Rodríguez and Evelyn Herrera announced their retirement from live musical performances, prioritizing family life over touring commitments. In their statement, the duo expressed that family is their "first ministry," reflecting a decision that privileges personal stability over professional visibility. However, both committed to continuing studio recording, maintaining their creative output in a format that allows for greater flexibility.

**Legacy**

Gente común, sueños extraordinarios reached number one on the Billboard Latin Pop Albums chart in September 2009 and was certified gold by the RIAA. A Dominican Christian act topping a mainstream chart had not happened before, and it is why the pair were invited to present at the Billboard Latin Music Awards in 2011.

Two songs got away from them, which is the part that lasts. El uno para el otro became standard repertoire at Christian weddings. Yo te extrañaré left the church altogether: it is sung at funerals across the Spanish-speaking world, often by people who do not know whose it is or that it is a Christian song.

The duo also left a second career behind it. Marcos Yaroide, the voice of the first era, who stepped away in 2006 to study theology, is now a central figure in Dominican worship in his own right.' WHERE slug = 'tercer-cielo';
